### What is the diameter of a binary tree?

The diameter is the number of edges on the longest path between any two nodes in the tree. This path may or may not pass through the root. It could connect two nodes deep within one subtree through a mid-level ancestor.

```java
package com.bank.model;

public class OrgNode {
    public String title;
    public OrgNode left;
    public OrgNode right;

    public OrgNode(String title) {
        this.title = title;
    }
}
```

### How does the efficient single-pass solution work?

Write a helper that returns the depth of a subtree. Before returning, update a shared maximum with the candidate diameter at the current node: `leftDepth + rightDepth`. The helper then returns `1 + max(leftDepth, rightDepth)` as the node's own depth.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.OrgNode;

public class TreeDiameter {
    private int maxDiameter = 0;

    public int diameter(OrgNode root) {
        maxDiameter = 0;
        depth(root);
        return maxDiameter;
    }

    private int depth(OrgNode node) {
        if (node == null) return 0;

        int left = depth(node.left);
        int right = depth(node.right);

        // Update diameter: longest path through this node
        maxDiameter = Math.max(maxDiameter, left + right);

        // Return depth of this subtree
        return 1 + Math.max(left, right);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        //       CEO
        //      /   \
        //    VP1   VP2
        //   /       \
        //  DIR1     DIR2
        //  /
        // MGR1
        OrgNode ceo = new OrgNode("CEO");
        OrgNode vp1 = new OrgNode("VP1");
        OrgNode vp2 = new OrgNode("VP2");
        OrgNode dir1 = new OrgNode("DIR1");
        OrgNode dir2 = new OrgNode("DIR2");
        OrgNode mgr1 = new OrgNode("MGR1");

        ceo.left = vp1; ceo.right = vp2;
        vp1.left = dir1; vp2.right = dir2;
        dir1.left = mgr1;

        TreeDiameter td = new TreeDiameter();
        System.out.println("Diameter: " + td.diameter(ceo) + " edges");
    }
}
```

```text
Diameter: 5 edges
```

### Why is the naive approach quadratic?

The naive approach computes depth independently at every node, which repeats work. For a skewed tree this becomes O(n^2). Fusing the depth computation with the diameter update makes each node's depth computed exactly once, keeping the total at O(n).

```java
// NAIVE O(n^2): recomputes depth at every node
public int diameterNaive(OrgNode root) {
    if (root == null) return 0;
    int throughRoot = depthSeparate(root.left) + depthSeparate(root.right);
    int leftDiam = diameterNaive(root.left);
    int rightDiam = diameterNaive(root.right);
    return Math.max(throughRoot, Math.max(leftDiam, rightDiam));
}

private int depthSeparate(OrgNode node) {
    if (node == null) return 0;
    return 1 + Math.max(depthSeparate(node.left), depthSeparate(node.right));
}
```

### What about the counting convention: edges vs nodes?

Some problems define diameter in nodes (count the nodes on the path) rather than edges. In edges, the formula is `leftDepth + rightDepth`. In nodes, it is `leftDepth + rightDepth + 1`. Always state which convention you are using.

```java
// Edges: leftDepth + rightDepth
maxDiameter = Math.max(maxDiameter, left + right);

// Nodes: leftDepth + rightDepth + 1
maxDiameter = Math.max(maxDiameter, left + right + 1);

// A single node: diameter in edges = 0, diameter in nodes = 1
```

### Why can the longest path skip the root?

The longest path does not have to go through the root. It could connect two nodes deep within the left subtree through a mid-level node. Evaluating the candidate diameter at every node catches paths that never touch the root.

```java
//       A
//      /
//     B
//    / \
//   C   D
//  /     \
// E       F
//
// Longest path: E -> C -> B -> D -> F = 4 edges
// This path goes through B, not through A
```

### What are the edge cases?

Empty tree (diameter 0), single node (diameter 0), and a completely linear tree (skewed) where the diameter equals the number of edges.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.OrgNode;

public class DiameterEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeDiameter td = new TreeDiameter();

        // Empty tree
        System.out.println("Empty: " + td.diameter(null));

        // Single node
        System.out.println("Single: " + td.diameter(new OrgNode("CEO")));

        // Skewed: CEO -> VP -> DIR -> MGR
        OrgNode skewed = new OrgNode("CEO");
        skewed.left = new OrgNode("VP");
        skewed.left.left = new OrgNode("DIR");
        skewed.left.left.left = new OrgNode("MGR");
        System.out.println("Skewed: " + td.diameter(skewed));
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: 0
Single: 0
Skewed: 3
```

### What is the relationship to Maximum Path Sum?

The diameter and maximum path sum problems share the same structure: a post-order recursion that returns one value to the parent while updating a global with a different value. In diameter, you return depth and update a sum of depths. In maximum path sum, you return the best single-branch gain and update a global with the best bending path. Recognizing this shared pattern is a senior-level observation.

```java
// Same structure in both problems:
private int helper(Node node) {
    if (node == null) return BASE;
    int left = helper(node.left);
    int right = helper(node.right);
    globalMax = Math.max(globalMax, COMBINE(left, right, node));
    return PROPAGATE(left, right, node);
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should be able to derive the O(n) solution by recognizing that the naive approach recomputes heights and explaining exactly where the repeated work happens. The dual role of the recursive helper, returning depth while updating diameter, is the key optimization.

In production at National Bank of India, this analysis runs on the org hierarchy to find the longest escalation chain between any two employees. An over-long chain slows approvals, so the service reports the diameter to the org-structure dashboard.

```java
// Report diameter as an org health metric
int chainLength = new TreeDiameter().diameter(orgRoot);
if (chainLength > MAX_ACCEPTABLE_CHAIN) {
    alertService.send("Org diameter " + chainLength
        + " exceeds threshold " + MAX_ACCEPTABLE_CHAIN);
}
```
